<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/living-room.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/living-room-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/living-room.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span>Questions answered</nav><span class="kicker fade-up">Answers</span><h1 class="fade-up" style="animation-delay:.08s">Oregon Insurance Appraisal Questions, Answered</h1><p class="lede fade-up" style="animation-delay:.15s">Plain answers to the questions we hear most about the appraisal process, costs, timing and Oregon’s rules and resources.</p><div class="btn-row fade-up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn sun" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn line-w" href="/reach-an-appraiser/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is insurance appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>A process in most property policies for settling disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an agreement signed by two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal the same as arbitration?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not exactly. Appraisal is limited to the amount of loss and is usually less formal. It doesn’t decide coverage or legal claims.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I demand appraisal if my claim was denied?</summary><div class="a"><p>Appraisal is designed for disputes about the amount of a covered loss. If the claim was denied outright, you will generally need to resolve coverage first, usually with an attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does my Oregon policy have an appraisal clause?</summary><div class="a"><p>Most homeowner and commercial property policies do. Look in the conditions section for a paragraph titled “Appraisal.” Oregon’s standard fire-policy provisions also include an appraisal process.</p></div></details></div><h2>Costs and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Each side usually pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee is usually split equally. Check your policy’s wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do appraisers charge?</summary><div class="a"><p>Flat fees, hourly rates or a percentage of the increase are all common. We put our fee in writing before any work starts.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the first conversation free?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We will look at what you have and tell you honestly whether appraisal is likely to help.</p></div></details></div><h2>Timing and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?</summary><div class="a"><p>Simple residential claims often finish within two to three months of both appraisers being named. Large or complex claims take longer.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Some policies set one; many don’t. But the policy’s deadline to file a lawsuit, commonly two years from the loss in Oregon, keeps running. Talk to an attorney if it is near.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I have to name my appraiser quickly?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many clauses give each side about 20 days after a written demand to name an appraiser. Check yours.</p></div></details></div><h2>Oregon rules and resources</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in Oregon?</summary><div class="a"><p>The Division of Financial Regulation, part of the Oregon Department of Consumer and Business Services. It licenses insurers, agents and public adjusters and accepts consumer complaints.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I file a complaint and use appraisal?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. A complaint with the Division of Financial Regulation addresses claim handling; appraisal addresses the amount of loss. They can run side by side.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the Oregon FAIR Plan?</summary><div class="a"><p>The Oregon FAIR Plan Association offers basic property coverage to owners who can’t find it in the regular market. Its policies have their own terms, including appraisal provisions.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does Oregon have special wildfire rules?</summary><div class="a"><p>After the 2020 Labor Day fires, Oregon adopted additional protections for wildfire policyholders in some situations, such as more time to collect replacement cost after a total loss in a declared emergency. Confirm current rules with the Division of Financial Regulation.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is earthquake damage covered?</summary><div class="a"><p>Not by a standard homeowner policy. Earthquake coverage is usually an endorsement or separate policy with a percentage deductible.</p></div></details></div><h2>Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you a public adjuster or a law firm?</summary><div class="a"><p>Neither. We provide appraisal, estimating and umpire services. We don’t negotiate claims outside appraisal and don’t give legal advice.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work outside the Portland area?</summary><div class="a"><p>Yes. We work statewide across all 36 counties.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for a first review?</summary><div class="a"><p>The insurer’s estimate, your contractor’s estimate, photos, the declarations page and any letters from the insurer.</p></div></details></div></article>
